      "review_id": "Ci9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2sxZk1YVnpkRlZxV0d4dkxWOTNVR1ZWZDNwMFdYYxAB",
      "author": "Prakash",
      "rating": 1,
      "relative_date": "8 months ago",
      "text": "Visited a skin doctor Nilofer Banu, who\nin my personal experience, seemed far more focused on selling medicines than treating patients. The prescription looked like a secret code that only her own clinic’s medical shop can “decode.” She even mentioned she writes this way because patients buy medicines outside at discounted rates as if that’s a crime.\n\nThe consultation felt rushed, the explanations were vague, and the attitude was downright dismissive. Basic concerns were brushed aside, and instead of clarity, I got a lecture for simply asking what the prescribed medicines were for. The clinic itself felt more like a business setup , long wait, quick check, and straight to the billing.\n\nIf creative handwriting and upselling were medical specialties, she’d be top-ranked. But for actual skin treatment? There are far better, more transparent doctors out there.\n\nTL;DR\n\nFelt like visiting a pharmacy disguised as a clinic , coded prescriptions, rushed consultation, dismissive attitude, and a focus on selling medicines. Not returning.",
      "has_owner_reply": false
    },
